## 7. Is the observer non-gating? (the facts, with lines)

- P03-S101 FACT (when the observer is reached; `signals/signal_processor.py`, A06's, interface only): each of the three calls of `_sr_observe` follows the signal's `PROCESSED` status write and the "placed" counters: L1543 to L1546, then L1550 (in `_admit_and_place`, L1277, after "Step 9: Success", L1541; comment L1548 to L1549 "runs only now that the order reached placement. Enqueues + returns immediately (never blocks/raises)"); L2290 to L2293, then L2296 (in `continue_from_gate`, L2010; comment L2295 "gate-release path; score N/A"); L2583 to L2586, then L2588 (in `continue_from_retest`, L2375). So the observer sees a candidate only after its order was placed and its signal marked `PROCESSED`; it is never asked before or during a placement decision.
- P03-S102 FACT (the seam, `_sr_observe`, L614 to L658; A06's code, recorded at the interface): returns at once if `self._sr_detector is None` (L637 to L638); inside `try`, imports `Candidate` from the package, builds it (L640 to L654) and calls `self._sr_detector.observe(cand)` (L655); `except Exception as exc` logs "sr_detector observe failed for %s: %s" at ERROR through `self._log.error` (L656 to L658; comment "Observer must never affect the pipeline (parity with shadow side-effects)"). A06 AUDIT EV-23 records these call sites as a MATCH ("no rejection or alteration through these call sites") and states that "never delay" depends on "A08's enqueue" (P07-S061).
- P03-S103 FACT and INTERPRETATION (A06 EV-23's A08 side: does `SRDetector.observe` return without waiting and without raising?): FACT: `observe` performs only the `_enabled` test and `put_nowait`, with `queue.Full` and every other exception caught and logged through the exception-safe `_safe_log` (L131 to L140, L311 to L317; P03-S037). INTERPRETATION (P03-S005 (a); P03-S038 to P03-S040): it does not wait for a free queue slot or for the analysis, which runs on the worker thread; it cannot raise for any `Candidate`, the only argument type its production callers pass (the one unguarded expression, `candidate.symbol` in the `Full` handler, exists on every `Candidate`). Its only costs on the caller's thread are the brief queue lock and, on a full queue or an unexpected error, one log call.
- P03-S104 FACT (does it alter the order path?): the observer's only effects are the `sr_detector_results` row and the in-memory telemetry counter (P03-S058, P03-S086); it calls no order, signal, trade, capital or kill-switch method; the `Candidate` it receives is a frozen dataclass (`sr_detector/models.py` L128, P01-S030) built by `_sr_observe` from values the processor has already used; nothing on the order path reads `sr_detector_results` or the counter (P03-S100: the readers are the A15 report, the A12 prune and the A08 backfill). A full queue, a fetch failure, a calculation exception or a write failure ends with a WARNING or ERROR line, a `FETCH_FAILED` row or no row, and never reaches the processor (P03-S038, P03-S044, P03-S055, P03-S086).
- P03-S105 INTERPRETATION (shared resource 1, the broker client and the rate limiter; interface: A01's `main.py`, A04's limiter): in live mode the fetch closure's handle is the trading process's own client (`_build_market_data_kite` returns `kite_client`, `main.py` L404 to L405; docstring L397 "Live: reuse kite_client (== adapter._kite)"), and each V1 fetch calls `rate_limiter.acquire("historical")` and then `historical_data(...)` on it (L428 to L437), on the "sr-detector" thread, with the process's `rate_limiter` (L3487). The worker makes up to three such calls per candidate (four with the anchors on). Whether a historical call shares a request budget, a lock or a connection with the order path's broker calls, and whether it could slow them, is A04's and A01's (A01 C38a, A01 B-11, A04 RL-05, A04 RL-11; part P02 is primary for them, P07-S020, P07-S021, P07-S041, P07-S130); the detector's code neither bounds nor measures it.
- P03-S106 INTERPRETATION (shared resource 2, the trading database; interface: A03's store): each V1 insert is one transaction begun with `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` on the worker's own connection to the same database file as the order path (P03-S087); SQLite allows "only one simultaneous write transaction" and an IMMEDIATE begin "might fail with SQLITE_BUSY if another write transaction is already active" (P03-S005 (g)); with `busy_timeout = 30000` (`core/state_store.py` L112) a writer that meets the lock waits. So while the observer's single INSERT and its `synchronous = FULL` commit hold the write lock, a write transaction of the order path begun at that moment waits for it. How the store handles the wait is A03's; whether any such wait occurred, and how long commits took on the production disk, is UNKNOWN / NOT ESTABLISHED.
- P03-S107 INTERPRETATION (shared resource 3, the interpreter): the worker's analysis (pivots, zones and scoring over up to 180 days of daily, 60-minute and 30-minute candles, P01-S102) runs as Python code in the trading process; in CPython "only one thread can execute Python code at once" (P03-S005 (b)), so while it computes it takes interpreter time that the order path's threads would otherwise have. The size of the effect is not established by any surviving evidence (UNKNOWN / NOT ESTABLISHED).
- P03-S108 FACT (start-up and shutdown; interface): the construction and `start()` run inside `main.py`'s `try` at L3492 to L3508, so a failure there leaves `sr_detector = None` and the boot continues (P03-S022); at shutdown `sr_detector.stop()` runs after `signal_proc.stop()` and waits at most 2.0 s for the worker (`main.py` L1690 to L1699; P03-S031, P03-S033), inside `try`.
- P03-S109 FACT (the committed state of the switch; values in the file only): `sr_detector.enabled: true` (`config/system_config.yaml` L509; comment "★ SNR-V1 shadow ARMED — committed true 28-Jun (durability > the old repo-default-false/safe-revert). Master flag (paper+live); false = not constructed") and `intraday_anchors_enabled: false` (L542). With these values `main.py` builds and starts the observer (L3491 to L3504) and every placement through the three call sites reaches `observe`. Whether the deployed configuration had these values is UNKNOWN / NOT ESTABLISHED (P07-S109).
- P03-S110 INTERPRETATION (the seam's own handler; A06's code, recorded only because it bears on "never raises into the pipeline"): the `except` of `_sr_observe` logs with `self._log.error(...)` and nothing guards that call (L656 to L658); an exception raised by that logging call itself would leave `_sr_observe` into the calling method after the `PROCESSED` write. This concerns the logger, not A08's code; A06 EV-23 records the call sites as a MATCH and this part does not reopen it.
- P03-S111 INTERPRETATION (summary, from P03-S101 to P03-S110): by its own code the V1 observer cannot reject, delay, size or alter a placement: it is reached only after the placement and the `PROCESSED` write, its hand-off does not wait, it raises nothing for a `Candidate`, and it writes nothing that the order path reads. The docstrings' "NEVER blocks/delays placement and NEVER raises into the pipeline" (`sr_detector/detector.py` L8 to L9) therefore holds for the call itself. Its possible effects on the order path are indirect, through resources it shares with it: the live broker client and rate limiter, the database's single write lock, and interpreter time; the code does not bound these, and their size in production is UNKNOWN / NOT ESTABLISHED (P03-AP01).
- P03-S112 UNKNOWN / NOT ESTABLISHED (runtime of the observer): whether the V1 observer ran in production, on which days, for how many candidates; how deep its queue became, whether any candidate was dropped, whether any worker error or write failure occurred; how long its fetches took and whether its fetches, inserts or computation ever delayed an order action. No log, database or journal of the legacy machines survives (Document 06, section 4.2). Legacy documents give accounts only (HISTORICAL, section 11.3: a report of 29 stored rows by 30-Jun-2026, P01-S139; "V1 shadow LIVE", `docs/SYSTEM_MAP.md` L1228).

## 8. The end-of-day backfill script

### 8.1 Module setup and the two helpers

- P03-S113 FACT (module setup, `scripts/sr_detector_backfill.py` L15 to L27): `ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent` (L20), the folder above `scripts/`; `ROOT` is put first on `sys.path` if absent (L21 to L22); `DB_PATH = ROOT / "data_store" / "trading_system.db"` (L24), the trading database located from the script's own place, not from the working directory; `_TERMINAL = {"CLOSED", "CLOSED_MANUAL", "CANCELLED", "FAILED"}` (L26 to L27, comment "Trade statuses that are terminal for outcome purposes").
- P03-S114 FACT (`_is_terminal`, L30 to L31; `_win_loss`, L34 to L41): `_is_terminal(status)` returns `bool(status) and (status in _TERMINAL or status.startswith("REJECTED"))`; `_win_loss(net_pnl)` returns `"UNKNOWN"` for `None`, `"WIN"` for a value above 0, `"LOSS"` below 0, otherwise `"FLAT"`.

### 8.2 `backfill(store, date_iso)`

- P03-S115 FACT (the selection, L44 to L50; docstring "Backfill outcomes for date_iso. Returns a small stats dict. Pure w.r.t. the store (testable with an in-memory StateStore)."): `rows = store.get_sr_results_for_backfill(date_iso)`; `stats = {"considered": len(rows), "filled": 0, "skipped_open": 0, "no_trade": 0}`. The store method (A03's, interface only; `core/state_store.py` L1997 to L2006; docstring "Return sr_detector_results rows for date_iso (by ts date) still awaiting outcome backfill (actual_result IS NULL)") runs `SELECT id, signal_id, symbol, direction FROM sr_detector_results WHERE date(ts) = ? AND actual_result IS NULL`. It does not filter on `detector_version`, `mode` or `structure_status`, so the V2 divert's rows (P03-S093) are selected as well as the V1 observer's.
- P03-S116 INTERPRETATION (what `date(ts)` selects; P03-S005 (f)): SQLite subtracts a `+05:30` suffix to compute UTC, so for a V1 row, whose `ts` carries the offset (P03-S085), `date(ts)` is the UTC date of the placement-time stamp; it equals the IST date for every stamp from 05:30 IST to midnight, a range that contains the committed session (`trading_hours` `market_open: "09:15"`, `market_close: "15:30"`, `config/system_config.yaml` L103 to L104), so V1 rows stamped at placements within the session are selected by that session's IST date (when placements can occur is A06's and A07's). A row stamped before 05:30 IST would be counted on the previous date (A03 H-11 records the same for `DATE()` of offset values). A V2 divert row, stamped naive (P03-S093), is selected by its wall-clock date.
- P03-S117 FACT (the trade lookup, L52 to L59): for each row, `sr_id = r["id"]`, `signal_id = r["signal_id"]` and `trade = store.fetch_one("SELECT entry_actual_price, exit_reason, net_pnl, status FROM trades WHERE signal_id = ? ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 1", (signal_id,))`: the newest `trades` row of the signal by `created_at`. The four columns and `signal_id`, `created_at` exist in `trades` (`core/schema.sql` L119, L131, L140, L146, L149, L152; A03's); `fetch_one` is the store's (`core/state_store.py` L564; A03's).
- P03-S118 FACT (the outcome written, by case, L61 to L93): (1) no trade row: `update_sr_outcome(sr_id, actual_fill=None, actual_result="NO_TRADE", win_loss="NO_FILL", pnl=None)` and `no_trade += 1` (L61 to L68; comment "Observer fires post-placement, so by EOD a missing trade is terminal."); (2) a status that `_is_terminal` rejects: `skipped_open += 1` and no write (L70 to L73); (3) `CLOSED` or `CLOSED_MANUAL`: `actual_result = trade["exit_reason"] or status`, `win_loss = _win_loss(trade["net_pnl"])`, `pnl = trade["net_pnl"]` (L75 to L78); (4) any other terminal status (comment "CANCELLED / FAILED / REJECTED*"): `actual_result = status`, `win_loss = "NO_FILL"`, `pnl = trade["net_pnl"]` (L79 to L82); in cases (3) and (4) `update_sr_outcome(sr_id, actual_fill=trade["entry_actual_price"], actual_result=..., win_loss=..., pnl=...)` and `filled += 1` (L84 to L91). The function returns `stats` (L93).
- P03-S119 FACT (the update, A03's, interface only; `core/state_store.py` L2008 to L2024; docstring "Backfill the EOD outcome columns for one sr_detector_results row."): `UPDATE sr_detector_results SET actual_fill = ?, actual_result = ?, win_loss = ?, pnl = ? WHERE id = ?`, each call in its own `with self.transaction()`; the UPDATE carries no `actual_result IS NULL` predicate.
- P03-S120 FACT (A03 U-05's A08 side, the status sets): the script treats as terminal `CLOSED`, `CLOSED_MANUAL`, `CANCELLED`, `FAILED` and every status beginning with `REJECTED` (L27, L31). The `trades.status` CHECK allows `PENDING`, `PENDING_FILL`, `OPEN`, `PARTIAL`, `EXITING`, `CLOSED`, `CLOSED_MANUAL`, `CANCELLED`, `FAILED`, `UNKNOWN_IN_FLIGHT` and `GLOB 'REJECTED*'` (`core/schema.sql` L152 to L161, NOT NULL), and the trigger `trg_trades_terminal_status_guard` treats `CLOSED`, `CLOSED_MANUAL`, `FAILED`, `CANCELLED` and `GLOB 'REJECTED*'` as terminal (L291 to L300). So the script leaves for a later run (skips as open) exactly `PENDING`, `PENDING_FILL`, `OPEN`, `PARTIAL`, `EXITING` and `UNKNOWN_IN_FLIGHT`, and its terminal set is the trigger's, as A03 U-05 records ("`sr_detector_backfill.py` L27, L31 = the trigger's set"). The status column comment (L152) omits `PENDING`, `CLOSED_MANUAL` and `UNKNOWN_IN_FLIGHT`, which the CHECK allows (A03's file).
- P03-S121 INTERPRETATION (the values the outcome columns can hold; from P03-S114, P03-S118): `win_loss` is `WIN`, `LOSS`, `FLAT`, `UNKNOWN` (a closed trade whose `net_pnl` is NULL) or `NO_FILL` (no trade, or a `CANCELLED`, `FAILED` or `REJECTED*` trade); `actual_result` is `NO_TRADE`, the trade's `exit_reason` (schema comment "SL_HIT/TGT_HIT/EOD/MANUAL/TIMEOUT/CIRCUIT_BREAKER", `core/schema.sql` L146) or, when that is NULL or empty, the status, or the non-closed terminal status; `actual_fill` is the trade's `entry_actual_price` (possibly NULL) except for `NO_TRADE` (NULL); for a non-closed terminal trade `pnl` is the trade's `net_pnl` while `win_loss` is `NO_FILL`. Whether a `CANCELLED`, `FAILED` or `REJECTED*` trade can carry a fill price or a P&L is the trade lifecycle's (A10, A03), not established here. None of these value sets is stated in a column comment (`core/schema.sql` L1284 to L1286).
- P03-S122 INTERPRETATION (the newest trade; from L57): when a signal has more than one `trades` row, only the one with the greatest `created_at` text decides the outcome; whether and when a signal can have several trade rows is A10's and A03's. A row whose signal had no trade at the moment of the run is closed as `NO_TRADE` permanently, on the comment's premise that the observer fires after placement (L62); a V2 divert row, written before placement (P03-S093), meets the same rule.
- P03-S123 INTERPRETATION (re-runs and idempotency; A03 Q-29's A08 side): every write sets `actual_result` to a non-NULL text (`NO_TRADE`; a status, which `_is_terminal` requires to be non-empty; or `exit_reason or status`), so a row written once is never selected again; a re-run for the same date considers only rows still NULL, which are the rows skipped as open and any rows a failed run did not reach, and it changes nothing else, as the test of a second run states (P03-S139). Each row's UPDATE is committed on its own, so a failure part-way leaves the earlier rows written and the rest NULL for a later run with that date. Two runs at the same time could both write one row (the UPDATE has no NULL predicate), with values read from the same trade row. A03 Q-29 records the selection and the per-row updates as a MATCH ("a re-run is safe").

